  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1143 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nNo significant changes in the forecast. Temperatures will trend a\ndegree or two warmer through the weekend with a slight uptick in\nthe chance of rain and thunderstorms today and Saturday.\n\nA weak northern latitude trough is in place across the central\nand northern Mississippi River Valley with a thermal ridge\npositioned to our east across most of the Southeastern US. A\nstrong ridge is centered west of the Continental Divide,\nextending into the Four-Corners Area. The Mid-South is positioned\nbetween these three features, but is mainly dominated by the\nridges to our west and east. As a result, temperatures will\nremain hot and likely trend slightly hotter today and Saturday.\nNevertheless, the presence of the northern latitude trough, along\nwith increasing surface moisture, will increase our chance of\nrain today and tomorrow. Storms will be diurnally driven and most\nlikely along and north of the Interstate 40 Corridor. A Heat\nAdvisory is in effect in the MS River Delta this afternoon, and\nfurther heat products will likely be warranted over the weekend\nand possibly into early next week. Any showers and thunderstorms\nmay limit the heat impact briefly and locally today and tomorrow,\nbut most of the Mid-South should expect to remain hot and dry.\n\nSunday into early next week, the trough to our north will\ndeamplify and shift east, limiting any forcing over the region as\nthe ridge out west expands east. Temperatures will maintain or\ntrend slightly warmer, but the chance of rain will decrease. For\nnow, our warmest day looks to be Tuesday, when afternoon highs\nare expected to be in the mid-90s. Heat indices near or slightly\nhigher than 105 degrees are likely area-wide. The need for Heat\nAdvisories will continue. Guidance is in fairly good agreement\nthat the ridge will retrograde Wednesday and after midweek, so\ntemperatures should trend cooler, but confidence in that solution\nis low.",
